Vasovagal syncope
Cardiovascular
Trigger-induced syncope, Prodrome of nausea/pallor/diaphoresis, Rapid recovery when supine, Hypotension/bradycardia on tilt-table

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: CDV-047
Reason for Visit: Sudden fainting episode while watching a medical documentary, causing a minor fall and extreme family anxiety.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and spouse
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage noted a transient loss of consciousness in a young adult. While the history strongly suggested a benign reflex faint, the sudden collapse caused significant distress to the spouse, and urgent in-person evaluation was necessary to assess for head trauma from the fall, confirm the patient had fully recovered neurological baseline, and rule out cardiogenic syncope or an active seizure before the next routine clinic hours.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "I was watching a surgery scene on TV, my stomach flipped, my vision tunneled, and the next thing I knew I was on the floor."
History of Present Illness: Patient (aged 24) reports a sudden, transient loss of consciousness reliably triggered by the sight of blood/medical procedures on television. The patient was standing in a warm living room at the time. Describes a distinct, classic prodrome: severe nausea, profound diaphoresis, and a sensation of extreme bodily warmth. This was immediately followed by a dimming or narrowing of vision ("tunneling") and a roaring/ringing sensation in the ears prior to the collapse. The spouse witnessed severe pallor and a limp fall to the carpet. The patient rapidly recovered consciousness within 20 seconds of adopting a supine posture. Currently complains of severe fatigue and a "washed-out" feeling. KEY SYMPTOMS: Sudden transient loss of consciousness reliably triggered by emotional distress or sight of blood. Distinct prodrome of nausea, profound diaphoresis. Dimming or narrowing of vision ('tunnelling') immediately prior to the event. Rapid recovery of consciousness upon adopting a supine posture. Post-event severe fatigue.
Functional Status: Fully alert, resting on the sofa, feeling drained.
Social/Safety Concerns: Spouse is highly anxious about the suddenness of the event.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP Supine: 110/70 | HR: 68 | Temp: 36.6 C | RR: 14 | SpO2: 100 percent | Wt: 65 Kg
General: Alert and oriented x3, slightly pale but well-perfused, no acute distress.
Physical Exam: Neurological: Normal power, sensation, and coordination. No focal deficits, no nystagmus. No tongue biting. No palpable head trauma or lacerations from the fall onto the carpet. Cardiovascular: Regular rate and rhythm. Heart sounds dual, no murmurs. Orthostatic vitals (supine to standing) are normal, showing no sustained hypotension or extreme tachycardia. Lungs: Clear bilaterally. DIAGNOSTIC TETRAD: Trigger-induced syncope (sight of blood/surgery), Prodrome of nausea/pallor/diaphoresis (by history), Rapid recovery when supine (witnessed), Hypotension/bradycardia on tilt-table (clinically assumed due to classic history).
Environmental Observations: Living room is quite warm; TV is paused on the medical documentary.
Medication Reconciliation: No routine medications.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Vasovagal Syncope - Status: Stable/Recovered
Differentials:

Seizure Disorder - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Presence of a classic autonomic prodrome (nausea, diaphoresis, tunnel vision), a specific emotional trigger (sight of blood), and the rapid, complete recovery without any postictal confusion or tonic-clonic movements.

Cardiogenic Arrhythmia - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The presence of a prolonged, distinct vagal prodrome rather than an unheralded, sudden "drop attack," and normal baseline cardiac auscultation.

Hypoglycemia - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Patient had eaten dinner recently, and rapid recovery occurred merely by changing posture (supine) without the administration of glucose.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with an absolutely textbook case of vasovagal (neurocardiogenic) syncope: a specific emotional trigger (sight of blood/medical procedure), a warm environment, a classic autonomic prodrome (nausea, tunnel vision, ringing ears), and a rapid recovery upon falling supine. The physical and neurological examinations are completely normal, effectively ruling out a traumatic brain injury from the fall or underlying structural heart disease. Urgent assessment was required purely to evaluate the fall, definitively exclude a cardiogenic cause, and provide immediate, expert reassurance to the highly distressed family, safely managing the patient at home.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Provided extensive reassurance regarding the completely benign nature of the vasovagal reflex. Advised adequate hydration (2-3 liters/day) and avoiding the specific visual triggers if possible. No formal investigations (like tilt-table testing) are indicated for a classic, uncomplicated first episode.
Positive Behavioral Change: Instructed the patient on "physical counter-pressure maneuvers." If they ever feel the nausea or tunnel vision starting again, they must immediately cross their legs tightly, tense all their leg and abdominal muscles, and sit down. By physically squeezing the blood out of the legs, they forcefully counteract the vagal drop in blood pressure. The reward is entirely aborting the faint before they lose consciousness.
Home-Based Services: None.
DME/Safety: None.
Follow-up: Routine GP follow-up only if episodes become frequent or occur without a prodrome.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

If the patient develops a severe headache, intractable vomiting, or becomes confused overnight (indicating a delayed bleed from the fall), the spouse must call an ambulance immediately.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is based on a classic clinical presentation; conditions are dynamic.
Clinical Course: Vasovagal syncope is a harmless overreaction of the nervous system. Recognizing the warning signs and sitting down immediately prevents injury.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Drink plenty of water. Tense your muscles and sit down immediately if you feel nauseous or dizzy. Seek emergency care if a severe headache develops tonight.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: Seeing the surgery on TV triggered a strong emotional reflex in your brain. This reflex inappropriately slowed your heart and relaxed your blood vessels, causing a sudden drop in blood pressure. Your brain didn't get enough oxygen for a few seconds, so you fainted. Falling flat fixed the gravity issue, which is why you woke up so fast. Your heart and brain are perfectly healthy.
Actionable Steps: Drink two large glasses of water now. If you ever feel that specific nausea and tunnel vision again, cross your legs tightly, tense your muscles, and sit on the floor immediately.

